6. WRONG CODE: long minus an inline address-of-subscript difference  [FIXED]
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
FIXED 2026-08-15 in two halves: a71fa8e (pass1) and da20bfe (pass2,
the long helper checking its operands before using them).  Both repro
forms print 77 under usersim, verified after da20bfe; cmd/od's odcvt
is restored to the original folded 2.11 text.

HALF FIXED 2026-08-15, commit a71fa8e - the pass1 half.  The scaling
shortcut for byte-sized elements returned before typing the
difference, so it kept its POINTER type; the enclosing subtraction
then took the pointer-arithmetic path and narrowed the long operand
to sixteen bits.  The tree is right now and char and int pointers
behave alike.

STILL WRONG: the answer is 65337 rather than 77, and it was 65337 for
int pointers before any of this - the char case only reached the same
bug.  Given

	(SUB:long (DEREF:long nd)
		  (SEXT:long (SUB:short (ADD buf 33) (REGVAR BC))))

c1 emits the long subtraction and never computes the right operand -
push hl with nothing loaded into hl - and reports no error.  Rules for
SEXT to long from INHL, INBC and INA all exist, so something matches
at the SUB:long and consumes the child without emitting it.  That is
where the remaining wrong number lives.

Found porting od (odcvt's digit-count arithmetic).  THIS ONE IS NOT
AN ERROR - IT COMPILES AND RUNS AND PRODUCES THE WRONG NUMBER, the
only silent-wrong-code case the 2.11 sweep found.  A pointer
difference whose left side is an inline &arr[CONST], subtracted into
a long, comes out zero.

	char buf[33];
	main()
	{
		char *b;
		long t, nd;

		b = &buf[10];
		nd = 100;
		t = nd - (&buf[33] - b);
		printf("%ld\n", t);
		return 0;
	}

Prints 0.  The answer is 77.  The compound form `t = nd;
t -= &buf[33] - b;` also prints 0.  Verified by running both under
usersim on 2026-08-15.

Correct, for calibration - each prints 77:

	e = &buf[33];  t = nd - (e - b);      named pointer, no inline &[]
	d = &buf[33] - b;  t = nd;  t -= d;   difference into an int first

In od's original function (many long locals, six register variables)
the related shapes flipped between "no rule" errors and this silent
zero as register allocation moved; cmd/od's odcvt is rewritten one
plain statement a time with an emitted-digit counter instead of the
closing pointer subtraction.  Until this is fixed, any pointer
difference written against an inline &arr[CONST] and mixed into long
arithmetic is suspect.

