18. WRONG CODE: __malloc loses its size parameter before growing  [FIXED]
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
FIXED as of the 2026-08-15 compiler: gaps/chain6.c grows the break
past the granted block (1adb to 2f84 for a 5.2K calloc), no stray
writes, chain intact - and cmd/less ch.c is the PRISTINE 2.11
original now, calloc and all.
Found porting less, and IT IS NOT LESS'S BUG - it is live in every
program linked against libc that allocates more than an arena block.
Ask calloc for ~5K from a frame with register struct pointers and
the arena grows by ONE BLOCK, 129 bytes, while the caller is handed
the full 5K - which sits beyond the break, and the segv checker
lights up on the first write past it.

The source is not the problem.  __malloc (lib/libc/malloc.c) rounds
its byte count at the top:

	nw = ((nw - 1 + sizeof(struct store)*2)/sizeof(struct store))
		* sizeof(struct store);

and grows by

	temp = ((nw+sizeof(struct store)-1+BLOCK)/BLOCK)*BLOCK;

For nw ~5166 that is ~5289 bytes of sbrk.  The observed growth is
exactly one BLOCK, which the arithmetic only produces for nw < 127:
by the time the grow expression runs, THE PARAMETER IS GONE -
clobbered somewhere across the allocator's search loops, a frame
with two register struct pointers and a static temp doing double
duty.  Root cause in c1 not yet isolated; the behavior and the
arithmetic pin the symptom to nw's death exactly.

Repro: gaps/chain6.c linked with the current libc
(or fresh-malloc.o, same result - this is live, not a stale object).
Prints the break before and after calloc(4, sizeof(struct){2 ptrs,
long, char[1024]}); the delta is 129 where ~5.2K was granted.
Verified under usersim 2026-08-15.

Until this is fixed: cmd/less carries a static buffer pool instead
of calloc (ch.c), and any port that mallocs kilobytes should suspect
its allocations before its logic.

