alarm on the way is worth recording: the first retest read as a
REGRESSION because the test harness did not answer less's ESC[6n
size probe, and every measurement was of a pager waiting for a
terminal - a harness for a program that queries its terminal must
BE a terminal.  cmd/less keeps the scalar pool anyway: on this
machine it is smaller and simpler, and the choice is now taste
rather than necessity.

Three shapes went wrong ONLY inside fch_get/ch_init's frames - the
minimal extractions (gaps/chain9.c, chainA.c) compile and run
correctly, so these are register-pressure- or frame-layout-
dependent, recorded here as observations with their live workaround
sites, which are also their repros:

	- the doubly-linked relink `bp->next->prev = bp->prev`
	  corrupted the anchor (cmd/less/ch.c, LRU relink through
	  plain temporaries now)
	- the circular-list anchor compare `bp != END_OF_CHAIN`
	  never fired in a three-register frame (ch.c walks are
	  counted by nbufs now)
	- subscripting a kilobyte-struct member array through a
	  struct pointer sprayed stores outside the object (ch.c
	  goes through a plain char pointer now)
	- pointer plus sizeof(kilobyte struct) advanced FOUR bytes
	  per step instead of 1030 in ch_init's three-register
	  frame, overlapping every pool buffer in a 44-byte span -
	  forward paging looked right only because each page
	  painted before the next read overwrote it (gaps/
	  stride.c prints stride=1030 standalone, correctly)

The family's common factor is the kilobyte struct: member offsets
past 255 and struct-pointer arithmetic with a large sizeof.  Four
local spellings were fixed one at a time and re-broke as unrelated
edits moved the frames, so cmd/less/ch.c now removes the CLASS: the
pool is parallel scalar arrays - a flat char array walked by char
pointer, word arrays indexed by small int, LRU by clock stamp
instead of a linked list.  When c1 claims this family, the test is
a struct-spelled pool put back in less - or any honest program
chaining kilobyte structs from a register-variable frame.

identifiers truncate at sixteen characters (limitation)
-------------------------------------------------------
c0 warns "symbol truncated" and keeps the first sixteen, so two
names agreeing there still match and the program is right - less's
END_OPTION_STRING compiled with warnings once its collisions were
renamed away.  asz has its own shorter horizon for labels.  Recorded
so the warning is recognized; the fix is shorter names, not a wider
symbol table, on a compiler that has to fit in 64k itself.

21. WRONG CODE, context-dependent: char compared with a negative
constant
----------------------------------------------------------------
Found by the range-audit sweep, retyping diff's option selector to
char: `opt == D_EDIT` with D_EDIT -1 stopped matching, and diff -e
silently dropped its ed command lines - the text survived, the 4a
and 2,3c lines did not.  Retyping opt back to int restores output
byte-identical to host diff.

The minimal extractions PASS standalone (gaps/charneg.c and the
enriched charneg2.c both print the right answer, verified under
usersim 2026-08-16), so like entry 19 this one lives in frame
context, and the live site is the repro: cmd/diff, diff.h's opt,
which stays int with the story beside it.  A sign-extension lost
somewhere between the byte load and the word compare, but only in
company.

The sweep's standing rule either way: a char compared against a
negative constant is entry-21 territory - keep such variables int
until this closes, however provable their range.

22. WRONG CODE: pointer pre-increment compared against an inline &arr[N]
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
Found porting v7 make's yacc parser skeleton (cmd/pwbmake/y.tab.c),
the state-stack overflow check:

	if (++yyps > &yys[YYMAXDEPTH])
		yyerror("yacc stack overflow");

The bound operand is a computed address of a LOCAL array - &yys[150]
lowers to (ADD (LOCALVAR frame) 300) - and the other is a pre-
increment pointer that also lives in the frame.  c1 computes the
bound into hl, then evaluates the pre-increment, which loads the
frame pointer into hl and overwrites the bound without saving it;
sbc hl,de then runs against the size constant 300 still sitting in
de, not the address.  The check fires on the very first push, so
every parse "overflows" before reading a token.

	int arr[150];
	main()
	{
		register int *p1, *p2, *p3, *p4, *p5;
		p1 = p2 = p3 = p4 = p5 = &arr[0];
		if (++p1 > &arr[150])	/* false: &arr[0] > &arr[150] is not */
			return 1;
		return 0;
	}

Returns 1, not 0 (verified against the .s: the bound is computed,
then ld l,(iy-6); ld h,(iy-5) clobbers it with p1, and sbc compares
against the leftover 300).  The bug wants the pre-increment operand
in the frame, which is why it hides in a register-poor function -
four register pointers hold the registers so p1 spills.  With the
array at file scope the bound is a SYMREF and the codegen orders the
operands the other way, correctly; with p1 in a register the bound
is pushed before the increment and popped after, also correctly.

Workaround in cmd/pwbmake: pre-compute the bound into a variable and
split the increment from the test:

	int *yyslim = &yys[YYMAXDEPTH];
	...
	++yyps;
	if (yyps > yyslim)
		yyerror("yacc stack overflow");
23. WRONG CODE: 32-bit `long` handling, four sites in the awk port
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
All four came out of porting 2.11BSD awk (cmd/awk), whose `obj` is a
`long` packing a cell pointer in the low word, a type in the next byte
and a subtype on top.  Every one is a place c1 drops or misroutes the
32 bits; all four have source-level workarounds, and awk now runs.

23a. a long narrowed with &0xffffL hands over the HIGH word
    (fixed: narrow through int / zero-extend through unsigned)

`(long)(x) & 0xffffL` returns the HIGH word, not the low, whenever x
is a runtime value with a nonzero high word.  `(int)(x & 0xffffL)`
alone is fine, and a constant folds correctly - the fault wants a
runtime long.  It bit both halves of awk's packed obj:

	objptr(o) = (cell *)((o) & 0xffffL)   -> high word (0x100)
	objmk(p,..)= (long)(p) & 0xffffL      -> the node's narg[0], not nobj

The objmk one is the subtle case: in nodetoobj, `(cell *)a->nobj`
lowered to a four-byte load of the struct field - nobj AND the
neighbouring narg[0] - and the &0xffff then returned narg[0] (0xd7bd)
instead of nobj (0xd78d).  The obj looked right in the debug print
because y=0x...d7bd prints the same low word objptr would; the pointer
was 48 bytes off.  That is why `print` dumped the text segment.

Workaround in awk.def: narrow objptr through int (which c1 does
truncate) and widen objmk through unsigned (which zero-extends):

	#define objptr(o) ((cell *)(int)(o))
	#define objmk(p,t,s) ((obj)(((long)(unsigned)(p)) | ((long)(t)<<16) | ((long)(s)<<24)))

gaps/longptrcast.c and longinit.c are the repros.

23b. a long global initialised from a shift folds to 16 bits
    (fixed: use literals)

	obj true = objmk(0, OBOOL, BTRUE);   /* (long)2<<16 | (long)1<<24 */

comes out two bytes and zero; the same value as a literal
(`obj zero = 0x01020000L`) is four bytes and right.  Workaround in
run.c: spell true/false as literals.

23c. the nested ternary in fcmp loses the sign of -1  (fixed: if/else)

	#define fcmp(a,b) ((a)>(b)?1:(a)<(b)?-1:0)

returns 255 for the -1 when the operands are 32-bit struct fields
(relop's `fcmp(objptr(x)->fval, objptr(y)->fval)`), so `1 > 3` came
out true and `1 < 3` false while `1 == 3` (which only needs the 0) was
right.  A standalone extraction with function parameters returns -1
correctly - the bug wants the struct-field operands, same as 23a.
Workaround: make fcmp a real function with if/else, in intmath.c.

23d. setsymtab's 2nd and 4th parameters read back garbage  [NOT REPRODUCIBLE]
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Checked 2026-08-19: the frame offsets (s=6, f=8 long, t=12), fentbxw/
fexitw, the qldiy/qstix long helpers and the 5-word argument push/pop
are all correct in the generated code, and a runtime guard on s and t
never fired across a battery of awk scripts.  A host build under
valgrind instead found four UNINITIALISED-obj reads in the awk source
(nodetoobj, arrayel, indirect, jump) that were themselves producing
the garbage-on-print ("funny variable"); those are fixed.  The frame
corruption itself does not reproduce on the current compiler, so it is
recorded rather than chased further.

The original observation: setsymtab(cbuf, s, 0L, CON|STR, symtab)
from yylexstr; s and t are right at the top (fprintf) but the stores
p->sval = s, p->tval = t read 0x0201 and 0x012a.  n, the long f, and
tab survive; only the two parameters either side of the long do not.

The common thread: every one of these is a `long` the compiler must
keep in two 16-bit halves, and c1 loses one half.  When a long is
involved, trust only the literal, the int-narrowed read, the
unsigned-widened pack, and an if/else compare until this entry closes.
